  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 118 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSunday...Upper ridge axis over the region will shift slightly to the \neast on Sunday, as another Pacific trough starts to strengthen and \ndip south along the West Coast. This will increase southwest flow \naloft, boosting temps across the area while at the same time aiding \nin moisture advection across the Four Corners. While much of the day \nwill be dry for the entire forecast area, by the evening isolated \nshowers creep back into the Continental Divide, which then becomes \nmore scattered in nature overnight as a closed low pushes onshore \ninto central CA. Plan on highs in the 70s for the high valleys, and \nupper 70s to upper 80s for the plains.\n\nMonday... A lot starts to happen on Monday, which will carry through \ninto mid week. First, the upper ridge over the central US weakens \neven more and continues to push to the east. The incoming Pacific \nclosed low system strengthens as it drops south across CA, while at \nthe same time Hurricane Polo moves onshore across the CA Baja and \ninto Mexico, feeding abundant moisture up into the Desert SW. Model \ntrends indicate that by Monday morning precip chances jump up \nconsiderably along the Continental Divide, then these high chances \nthen spread from west to east through the day, increasing to \nscattered pops for the eastern plains by the night. This trend seems \nreasonable at this time, even with variables that could crop up, and \nQPF amounts are ranging around .5 inch of rain along the Continental \nDivide, and a .1 to .2 for the rest of the higher terrain, through \nMonday. The plains will likely see very low amounts initially Monday \nevening. As for temps, increased cloud cover and incoming precip \nwill cool the higher elevations and valleys a bit, but it should be \none more warm day across the eastern plains. Therefore, look for \nhighs in the 60s for the high valleys, and mid 70s to lower 80s for \nthe plains.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...There are some model differences showing up \nby Tuesday, though there is some agreement on sweeping the upper low \neast into AZ where it merges with the remnants of Hurricane Polo on \nTuesday, then Wednesday the track of this low is then up in the air. \nRegardless, the potential for a good amount of additional rainfall \nfor all of the forecast area through midweek is there. High temps \nboth days are anticipated to be much cooler and below seasonal \nnormals.\n\nThursday...The NBM paints Thursday as a few degrees warmer as the \nsystem exits the area and precip chances dwindle. This will be \nhighly dependent on track of the upper low, with some models hinting \nat the low lingering in the area for another 24 hours. Stay tuned as \nthis solution will likely go through some adjustments in the \nfollowing days. Moore",
